🚩 picoCTF - Disk, disk, sleuth! (Forensics)

📊 Challenge Information

Category: Forensics

Difficulty: Easy

Tools used: mmls, fls, icat (The Sleuth Kit)

🕵️‍♂️ Investigation Process

Method 1: The Professional Forensic Way (Using Sleuth Kit)

Decompress the image:

gunzip dds1-alpine.flag.img.gz


Find the partition offset: We check the partition table to locate the Linux partition start sector:

mmls dds1-alpine.flag.img


The main Linux partition starts at sector offset 2048.

List files inside the partition: We use fls to recursively search for files in this partition by pointing to the offset 2048:

fls -r -o 2048 dds1-alpine.flag.img


This scan reveals a file named flag.txt with its corresponding inode number (e.g., 18281).

Extract file content: Use icat with the offset and inode number to read the flag directly from the raw disk image without mounting it:

icat -o 2048 dds1-alpine.flag.img 18281


Method 2: The Quick Way (Using Strings)

Since this is an unencrypted, simple challenge disk, we can also extract ASCII strings and filter for the flag:

strings dds1-alpine.flag.img | grep "picoCTF"


